@@ -0,0 +1,170 @@
+# Levenshtein sequence reconstruction generator (arXiv:2211.08812)
+
+Status: the generator and all local gates pass, but the required external oracle
+hardening is **not complete**. OpenRouter returned HTTP 403 “key limit exceeded”
+before any model produced an answer. The script-owned error transcripts are retained;
+they are not counted as model failures and do not establish a hardened verdict.
+
+| profile field | value |
+|---|---|
+| Track | B — an efficient algorithm is disclosed and measured |
+| Native domain | algebra |
+| Object regime | finite field |
+| Computational core | linear algebra |
+| Certificate | exact symbolic binary codeword, serialized as fixed-width hex |
+| Intuition | invariant: global XOR cancellation followed by a Hamming syndrome |
+| Domain essentiality | native; no reduction |
+
+## Problem and trust model
+
+The source is Junnila, Laihonen, and Lehtilä, [*The Levenshtein's Sequence
+Reconstruction Problem and the Length of the List*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.08812).
+Section 1 defines the candidate list as
+`T(Y) = C ∩ ⋂_{y∈Y} B_t(y)`. An instance supplies a binary Hamming code `C` and an
+unordered set of distinct received words `Y`; the solver returns any codeword within
+Hamming distance `t` of every received word. Verification recomputes the GF(2)
+syndrome and every exact Hamming distance. It accepts any valid witness and never
+reads the planted answer.
+
+Generation is inverse, not solve-and-publish. A uniform Hamming codeword is sampled
+first. Every channel then receives exactly `t=e+ell` substitutions. Error masks are
+composed as XOR-zero quadruples and one constant-weight triple with singleton XOR,
+then shuffled. This proves the planted word valid by construction. The generator also
+checks the deterministic inequality of Section 6, Theorem 28, with `k=e=1` on every
+G1 instance.
+
+## Why Track B
+
+The paper does not claim computational hardness. Section 6 instead gives the
+coordinatewise majority algorithm and states its optimal complexity `Theta(Nn)`.
+The shipping setting has `n=255`, `N=43`: the reference implementation performs
+10,990 counted operations (10,965 channel-bit inspections plus syndrome work), solves
+8/8 instances as expected, and took 0.194 seconds total in the recorded run. Random
+corruptions would therefore be a false Track A family.
+
+The planted composition has a shorter route: XOR the odd number of received words,
+compute the Hamming syndrome of the aggregate with eight fixed parity masks, and
+correct that one coordinate. This takes 68 counted whole-word operations. The
+mechanical route is roughly 159 times larger, while the compact route remains under
+the no-tool cap. The challenge is noticing the invariant; the oracle evidence needed
+to show that models actually miss it is still unavailable because of the credential
+failure.
+
+Easy regimes deliberately avoided are `t<=e` (Section 1 says one channel suffices),
+the high-channel list-size bounds in Theorems 4 and 6, and pretending the Section 6
+majority decoder does not exist. Our `t>e`, correlated adversarial outputs stay in the
+paper's native model; Theorem 28, rather than a probabilistic independence claim,
+certifies the majority behavior.
+
+## Worked demo
+
+With `seed=31415`, `n=15`, `N=11`, and `t=3`, the complete received set is:
+
+```text
+6ba7  0b57  2b87  0927  494f  0b83
+4ba3  4915  4b35  0947  4b5f
+```
+
+Coordinates are 1-based from the least significant bit. The Hamming code condition
+is that the XOR of all indices carrying a `1` is zero. XOR cancellation and one-error
+decoding give `<answer>4b07</answer>`.
+
+```text
+verify(inst, "4b07") -> (True, "ok")
+verify(inst, "4b06") -> (False, "word is not in the Hamming code (syndrome 1)")
+```
+
+A person can solve this demo on paper: it needs 11 four-hex-digit XORs and four parity
+checks (24 counted operations). Exhaustive verification confirms that `4b07` is the
+only valid codeword among all 2,048 demo codewords.
+
+## Difficulty presets
+
+| preset | n | channels N | t | reference bit scan | compact operations | status |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| demo | 15 | 11 | 3 | 165 | 24 | hand example; harden skips it |
+| easy | 31 | 15 | 5 | 465 | 31 | local gates pass; oracle unavailable |
+| medium | 127 | 31 | 7 | 3,937 | 53 | local gates pass; oracle unavailable |
+| hard | 255 | 43 | 9 | 10,965 | 68 | shipping candidate; oracle unavailable |
+
+If the named hard rung is solved, `escalate()` grows the haystack at the fixed
+255-bit answer length through 83, 163, and 263 channels (108, 188, and 288 compact
+operations). No preset was rejected by a local gate.
+
+## Gate results
+
+| gate | measured result |
+|---|---|
+| G1 | 16/16 planted witnesses verify; Theorem 28 inequality 16/16 |
+| G2 | 8 corruption classes rejected with 8 distinct reasons |
+| G3 | tagged prose/fence round-trip passes; answer is JSON-native |
+| G4 | 0 hits / 200,000 uniform Hamming-code candidates |
+| G5 | shipping density 0/200,000; demo exactly 1/2,048; reference 10,990 ops |
+| G6 | four attacks each 0/8; disclosed majority reference 8/8 |
+| G7 | `n=511` scale instance builds and verifies; candidate space increases |
+| G8 | 80/80 invariant-key checks, 80/80 carried witnesses, 20/20 distinct seeds |
+| G9(c) | 66 JSON characters/tokens upper bound, 255 bit atoms, 68 operations |
+
+G4/G5 sample from the strongest free prior stated in the problem: uniformly from all
+codewords satisfying the public Hamming parity checks. Zero observed hits is an upper
+resolution statement at 200,000 samples, not proof that the answer is unique at the
+shipping setting and not a confidence bound under arbitrary model priors.
+
+## Oracle loop and G9 arms
+
+The bare run stopped at the first preset because every redraw was an API error. These
+rows are preserved in `llm_loop_transcript.jsonl`; `solved=error` is not `False`.
+
+| preset | seed | model | result | reason |
+|---|---:|---|---|---|
+| easy | 710239278 | Gemini 3.1 Pro Preview | error | HTTP 403 key limit |
+| easy | 836773081 | Grok 4.6 | error | HTTP 403 key limit |
+| easy | 36248880 | Gemini 3.1 Pro Preview | error | HTTP 403 key limit |
+| easy | 1946720519 | Gemini 3.1 Pro Preview | error | HTTP 403 key limit |
+
+| G9 arm | solved / valid attempts | API errors | verdict |
+|---|---:|---:|---|
+| bare | 0 / 0 | 4 | unavailable |
+| structural hint | 0 / 0 | 4 | unavailable |
+| placebo hint | 0 / 0 | 4 | unavailable |
+
+`hinted - placebo` is therefore undefined, and no conclusion about the declared
+invariant can be drawn from G9. The hint names only the global XOR invariant; it does
+not provide the correction procedure.
+
+## Use
+
+```python
+import importlib.util
+
+spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location(
+    "g", "results/2211.08812/gen_2211_08812.py"
+)
+g = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
+spec.loader.exec_module(g)
+
+inst = g.make_instance(seed=7, **g.DIFFICULTY[g.SHIPPING_DIFFICULTY])
+statement = g.render(inst)
+candidate = g.parse_answer("<answer>" + inst["answer"] + "</answer>")
+assert g.verify(inst, candidate) == (True, "ok")
+```
+
+From the repository root, after a valid oracle hardening run exists:
+
+```bash
+bash scripts/emit.sh 2211.08812 20 hard
+```
+
+## Caveats
+
+The family becomes easy with a sandbox: both majority and the hidden XOR route are
+fast, which is precisely the Track B claim. The mask identities create higher-order
+correlations; a stronger algebraic detector than the four tested attacks may expose
+them. We did not run SAT/SMT, information-set decoding, belief propagation, or a
+learned distinguisher because the reference majority algorithm already solves every
+instance and belongs outside the failing attack panel on Track B. The canonical key
+uses the complete multiset of per-row Hamming-distance profiles. It is invariant under
+all tested Hamming-code symmetries but can over-collapse rare nonisomorphic sets with
+the same metric profiles. Finally, the 255-bit witness sits one atom below the G9 cap,
+and the required four-vendor no-tool claim remains unverified until OpenRouter quota is
+restored and all three arms are rerun.
