957:Observe that~\eqref{Eq_sum_condition} allows us to estimate the accuracy of $\bz$. In particular, if $k \leq e$, then $d(\bx, \bz) \leq k \leq e$ and the word $\bz$ can be decoded to $\bx$ as $C$ is an $e$-error-correcting code. Furthermore, if $k > e$, then $\bx \in C \cap B_k(\bz)$ and the decoding algorithm outputs a list of words containing $\bx$. Moreover, the size of the list is at most $\max_{\bu \in \F^n} |C \cap B_k(\bu)|$, which is closely related to the traditional list decoding (see~\cite{Guruswamin_kirja}). In conclusion, the theorem gives us a condition guaranteeing that the transmitted word can be decoded uniquely or with certain accuracy.
