+This is deliberately a Track B family and becomes easy with a sandbox: both majority
+and the hidden XOR route are fast. The 68-operation count treats a fixed-width
+255-bit XOR as one exact word operation; counting hexadecimal digits instead would
+make the manual route substantially longer, so the G9 interpretation is conservative
+only at the machine-word abstraction used by the module. The balanced construction
+has higher-order correlations; although raw weight, medoid, random restart,
+hex-digit plurality, and uncorrected XOR all fail, a stronger algebraic or learned
+distinguisher may expose them. SAT/SMT, information-set decoding, and belief
+propagation were not run because the disclosed majority algorithm already solves the
+class and belongs outside Track B's failing panel.
+
+The canonical key uses the complete multiset of per-row Hamming-distance profiles.
+It is invariant under every tested output reorder, codeword translation, and linear
+coordinate automorphism, but rare nonisomorphic output sets can share those profiles.
+No `gvlib` helper is needed: Python integer XOR, bit counts, and syndromes provide
+exact finite-field verification throughout. Most importantly, the cross-vendor
+no-tool claim remains unverified until OpenRouter quota is restored and all three
+arms are rerun.
