+Section 1 defines the feasible list as `T(Y) = C ∩ ⋂_{y∈Y} B_t(y)`. An instance
+hands the solver a binary Hamming code `C` and an unordered set of distinct received
+words `Y`; the solver returns any codeword within Hamming distance `t` of every
+received word. Verification recomputes the GF(2) syndrome and every Hamming distance
+using exact integer operations. It accepts any valid witness and never reads the
+planted answer.
+
+Generation is inverse, not solve-and-publish. A uniform codeword `x` is sampled
+first. The generator builds a binary error matrix with every row of weight exactly
+`t`. Every column has even weight strictly below half the channels except one
+near-half column of odd weight. Greedily assigning each column to the currently
+least-loaded rows makes all row weights equal; column parity proves that XORing every
+error row gives exactly the odd singleton. Hence all received words are exactly `t`
+from `x`, their bitwise majorities equal `x`, and XORing the odd number of received
+words gives a word one bit from `x`. The latter is corrected by the Hamming syndrome.
 
-Generation is inverse, not solve-and-publish. A uniform Hamming codeword is sampled
-first. Every channel then receives exactly `t=e+ell` substitutions. Error masks are
-composed as XOR-zero quadruples and one constant-weight triple with singleton XOR,
-then shuffled. This proves the planted word valid by construction. The generator also
-checks the deterministic inequality of Section 6, Theorem 28, with `k=e=1` on every
-G1 instance.
+Section 6, Theorem 28 supplies a second exact uniqueness argument. Its inequality
+with `k=e=1` holds on every tested instance, so every feasible codeword is within one
+bit of the same majority word. A distance-three Hamming code has at most one such
+codeword, while construction supplies one. This proves that shipping has exactly one
+valid answer without solving it.
 
 ## Why Track B
 
-The paper does not claim computational hardness. Section 6 instead gives the
-coordinatewise majority algorithm and states its optimal complexity `Theta(Nn)`.
-The shipping setting has `n=255`, `N=43`: the reference implementation performs
-10,990 counted operations (10,965 channel-bit inspections plus syndrome work), solves
-8/8 instances as expected, and took 0.0046 seconds total in the recorded run. Random
-corruptions would therefore be a false Track A family.
