+Section 1 defines the feasible list as `T(Y) = C ∩ ⋂_{y∈Y} B_t(y)`. An instance
+hands the solver a binary Hamming code `C` and an unordered set of distinct received
+words `Y`; the solver returns any codeword within Hamming distance `t` of every
+received word. Verification recomputes the GF(2) syndrome and every Hamming distance
+using exact integer operations. It accepts any valid witness and never reads the
+planted answer.
+
+Generation is inverse, not solve-and-publish. A uniform codeword `x` is sampled
+first. The generator builds a binary error matrix with every row of weight exactly
+`t`. Every column has even weight strictly below half the channels except one
+near-half column of odd weight. Greedily assigning each column to the currently
+least-loaded rows makes all row weights equal; column parity proves that XORing every
+error row gives exactly the odd singleton. Hence all received words are exactly `t`
+from `x`, their bitwise majorities equal `x`, and XORing the odd number of received
+words gives a word one bit from `x`. The latter is corrected by the Hamming syndrome.
 
