codex
Implemented and locally verified the Track B generator, but external hardening remains blocked by exhausted OpenRouter quota.

The [paper](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2211.08812) explicitly gives an optimal \(\Theta(Nn)\) majority decoder, ruling out Track A. The generator instead plants a balanced error matrix with a singleton XOR invariant, giving a 68-operation compact route versus 10,990 reference operations.

Key results:

- All local gates G1–G9(c) pass.
- Guessing: 0/200,000; exact density \(1/2^{247}\).
- Five adversarial attacks: 0/8 each.
- Reference majority decoder: 8/8, as expected for Track B.
- Deterministic fuzzing: 2,000/2,000 instances passed.
- A hexadecimal-plurality weakness in the prior construction was discovered and eliminated.

Deliverables:

- [gen_2211_08812.py](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2211.08812/gen_2211_08812.py)
- [selftest_report.json](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2211.08812/selftest_report.json)
- [README.md](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2211.08812/README.md)
- [llm_loop_transcript.jsonl](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2211.08812/llm_loop_transcript.jsonl)
- [g9_hinted_transcript.jsonl](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2211.08812/g9_hinted_transcript.jsonl)
- [g9_placebo_transcript.jsonl](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2211.08812/g9_placebo_transcript.jsonl)

Every revised oracle call returned HTTP 403 “key limit exceeded.” These are correctly recorded as errors rather than model failures, so no hardened verdict is claimed. Restoring the OpenRouter key’s total limit and rerunning `harden.py` is the sole remaining requirement.
