 NOTES = r"""
-Paper grounding and Step 0.  Section 1 fixes the native reconstruction object:
+Paper grounding and Step 0. Section 1 defines the native feasible list as
 T(Y)=C intersected with all radius-t Hamming balls around the distinct channel
-outputs, where t=e+ell>e.  Sections 2--5 study information-theoretic list size,
-not computational search hardness.  The easy regimes that matter here are stated
-explicitly: one channel suffices when t<=e; Theorems 4 and 6 bound list size once
-the number of channels crosses V(n,ell-1)+1; and Section 6 gives the well-known
-majority algorithm with optimal Theta(Nn) time.  Theorem 28 supplies a deterministic,
-exactly checkable inequality guaranteeing that majority lies within k of the
-transmitted word.  A random-corruption planted family would therefore fail Track A.
+outputs, with t=e+ell>e. Sections 2--5 are extremal/list-size results, not a
+computational hardness theorem. The easy facts that control this generator are
+explicit: Section 1 says one channel suffices for t<=e; Theorems 4 and 6 bound
+the list in high-channel regimes; and Section 6 states that coordinatewise
+majority costs optimal Theta(Nn). Theorem 28 gives an executable inequality
+certifying that every feasible transmitted word is within k of the same
+majority word. Therefore a Track-A claim would be false, while a no-tool Track-B
+compression problem remains meaningful.
 
-Generation and certificate.  C is the binary Hamming code of length 2^r-1, whose
-parity-check columns are the nonzero r-bit coordinate labels; it has minimum
-distance 3 and e=1.  A uniform codeword x is sampled first.  Every channel error
-mask has exactly the public weight t=e+ell.  The error matrix is built by assigning
-an even, near-half column weight to every coordinate except one coordinate with an
-odd, near-half weight.  A least-loaded-row construction makes every row have weight
-exactly t.  Consequently every coordinate has a strict correct majority while the
-XOR of all error rows is exactly the singleton odd column.  Thus all outputs are
-distinct and exactly t from x, Theorem 28 applies with k=1, and XOR of all odd-many
-outputs is one bit from x.  The planted answer is never found by solving the
-instance.
+Generation and certificate. C is a labeled linear subcode of the binary Hamming
+code of length 2^r-1. Its only free non-parity coordinates are the high-order
+4p-1 bits printed as the first p hexadecimal digits; all other data coordinates
+are zero, and the r parity coordinates are filled by the standard Hamming
+syndrome. This subcode has minimum distance three: it inherits distance at least
+three from the Hamming code, and two suitable payload coordinates plus their
+power-of-two XOR parity coordinate give a weight-three codeword. Thus e=1.
 
-Track B and attacks.  The paper's majority decoder is the successful reference
-algorithm, not a failed Track-A attack.  Shipping majority reads Nn=10,965 bits;
-the alternative identity needs N whole-word XORs, r fixed parity masks, and one
-bit flip.  All received rows have the same distance t from x, every bit is close
-to a tie, and public order is randomized.  The adversary panel tests raw-weight
-outliers, a Hamming medoid with one-error decoding, uniform codeword restarts,
-hexadecimal-digit plurality, and the tempting but invalid raw XOR answer.  Exact
-timings and operation counts are produced by selftest().
+A uniformly random payload and its codeword x are sampled first. Every channel
+error has exactly t bits. The error-incidence matrix is row-regular; all payload
+columns have the same even near-half degree, while non-payload columns have that
+degree or one more. Columns are assigned with random tie-breaking to the least
+loaded rows, making the rows exchangeable and exactly regular. Every column is
+strictly below half the channels, so coordinatewise majority is x. Theorem 28
+with k=e=1 then forces every feasible codeword within one bit of x; distance
+three makes the feasible answer unique. Construction supplies that answer, so
+no generated instance is solved during generation.
+
+Track B and attacks. The full paper algorithm reads N*n=2,805 bits at shipping;
+because this subcode is systematic, the strongest reference implementation can
+skip fixed coordinates and reads N*(4p-1)=1,045 bits. Both are disclosed, and
+the latter is timed in selftest. The compact identity instead XORs only the p
+displayed prefix digits down the N rows. Error parity is even there and N is
+odd, so the result is the payload in p*(N-1)=240 single-digit XORs. This count
+does not pretend a 255-bit machine operation is a hand operation.
+
+The failed panel tests a raw-weight outlier, a Hamming medoid, 256 uniform
+payload restarts, hexadecimal-digit plurality, and a tempting three-row partial
+XOR. All error rows have equal weight, row order is shuffled, and payload error
+columns are close to tied; plants and channel decoys are not drawn from visibly
+different row distributions. Exact costs and success counts are produced by
+selftest().
 """.strip()
 
 
