diff --git a/README.md b/README.md
deleted file mode 100644
index 8437eda88cd9f63210db64c86205002710e2ba8c..0000000000000000000000000000000000000000
--- a/README.md
+++ /dev/null
@@ -1,187 +0,0 @@
-# Levenshtein sequence reconstruction generator (arXiv:2211.08812)
-
-Status: the revised generator passes every local gate, but the required external
-oracle hardening is **not complete**. OpenRouter returned HTTP 403 “key limit
-exceeded” on every revised-family draw. Those rows are retained as errors, not
-counted as model failures, and no hardened claim is made.
-
-| profile field | value |
-|---|---|
-| Track | B — an efficient reference algorithm is disclosed and measured |
-| Native domain | algebra |
-| Object regime | finite field |
-| Computational core | linear algebra |
-| Certificate | exact symbolic binary codeword, serialized as fixed-width hex |
-| Intuition | invariant: global XOR cancellation followed by a Hamming syndrome |
-| Domain essentiality | native; no reduction |
-
-## Problem and trust model
-
-The source is Junnila, Laihonen, and Lehtilä, [*The Levenshtein's Sequence
-Reconstruction Problem and the Length of the List*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.08812).
-Section 1 defines the feasible list as `T(Y) = C ∩ ⋂_{y∈Y} B_t(y)`. An instance
-hands the solver a binary Hamming code `C` and an unordered set of distinct received
-words `Y`; the solver returns any codeword within Hamming distance `t` of every
-received word. Verification recomputes the GF(2) syndrome and every Hamming distance
-using exact integer operations. It accepts any valid witness and never reads the
-planted answer.
-
-Generation is inverse, not solve-and-publish. A uniform codeword `x` is sampled
-first. The generator builds a binary error matrix with every row of weight exactly
-`t`. Every column has even weight strictly below half the channels except one
-near-half column of odd weight. Greedily assigning each column to the currently
-least-loaded rows makes all row weights equal; column parity proves that XORing every
-error row gives exactly the odd singleton. Hence all received words are exactly `t`
-from `x`, their bitwise majorities equal `x`, and XORing the odd number of received
-words gives a word one bit from `x`. The latter is corrected by the Hamming syndrome.
-
-Section 6, Theorem 28 supplies a second exact uniqueness argument. Its inequality
-with `k=e=1` holds on every tested instance, so every feasible codeword is within one
-bit of the same majority word. A distance-three Hamming code has at most one such
-codeword, while construction supplies one. This proves that shipping has exactly one
-valid answer without solving it.
-
-## Why Track B
-
-The paper proves information-theoretic list bounds, not computational hardness.
-Section 6 explicitly gives coordinate-wise majority in optimal `Theta(Nn)` time.
-At shipping, majority plus Hamming decoding performs 10,990 counted operations
-(10,965 channel-bit inspections plus syndrome work), solves 8/8 as expected, and
-took 0.0070 seconds total in the recorded run. Random independent corruptions would
-therefore be a false Track A family.
-
-The compact route is structural: XOR the 43 whole received words, compute eight
-parity checks, and correct the indicated coordinate. It uses 68 counted exact
-word-level operations. Without noticing the cancellation, the received bits are all
-close to tied and the solver must carry out the paper's mechanical majority scan.
-Easy regimes explicitly avoided are `t<=e` (Section 1 says one channel suffices) and
-pretending that the high-channel list bounds of Theorems 4 and 6, or the Section 6
-decoder, do not exist.
-
-An earlier sparse-error revision was genuinely trivial: a Gemini oracle solved the
-medium instance by hexadecimal-digit plurality, and that attack solved 100/100 old
-hard instances locally. Its script-owned evidence is preserved in
-`prebalance_llm_loop_transcript.jsonl`. The revised near-half columns reduce that
-same attack to 0/100 medium and 0/100 hard; it is now part of G6.
-
-## Worked demo
-
-For `seed=31415`, `n=15`, `N=11`, and `t=5`, the complete received set is:
-
-```text
-4a6b  1106  6f14  1806  0505  1747
-4a49  6767  6bab  6bbf  4800
-```
-
-Coordinates are 1-based from the least significant bit. XOR cancellation and
-one-error Hamming decoding give `<answer>4b07</answer>`.
-
-```text
-verify(inst, "4b07") -> (True, "ok")
-verify(inst, "4b06") -> (False, "word is not in the Hamming code (syndrome 1)")
-```
-
-A person can solve this demo on paper: it needs 11 four-hex-digit XORs and four
-parity checks (24 counted word operations). Exhaustive verification finds exactly
-one valid codeword among all 2,048 demo codewords.
-
-## Difficulty presets
-
-| preset | n | channels N | t | majority bit reads | compact ops | status |
-|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
-| demo | 15 | 11 | 5 | 165 | 24 | hand example; harden skips it |
-| easy | 31 | 15 | 11 | 465 | 31 | local gates pass; revised oracle unavailable |
-| medium | 127 | 31 | 55 | 3,937 | 53 | local gates pass; revised oracle unavailable |
-| hard | 255 | 43 | 117 | 10,965 | 68 | shipping candidate; revised oracle unavailable |
-
-If hard is solved, `escalate()` keeps the 255-bit answer fixed and raises only the
-number of channels through 83, 163, 263, and 275 (108, 188, 288, and 300 compact
-operations), then returns `cap_bound`. This grows the haystack rather than the
-witness and stops exactly at the G9 effort cap.
-
-## Gate results
-
-| gate | measured result |
-|---|---|
-| G1 | 16/16 planted witnesses verify; Theorem 28 inequality 16/16 |
-| G2 | 8 corruption classes rejected with 8 distinct reasons |
-| G3 | tagged prose/fence round-trip passes; answer is JSON-native |
-| G4 | 0 hits / 200,000 uniform Hamming-code candidates |
-| G5 | exactly 1 valid shipping word in `2^247` (`4.42e-75`); sampled 0/200,000; reference cost 10,990 ops |
-| G6 | five attacks each 0/8; disclosed majority reference 8/8; compact route 8/8 |
-| G7 | `n=511` instance builds and verifies; candidate space increases |
-| G8 | 80/80 invariant-key checks, 80/80 carried witnesses, 20/20 distinct seeds |
-| G9(c) | 66 serialized characters/tokens upper bound, 255 bit atoms, 68 operations |
-
-G4/G5 sample from the strongest free prior stated in the problem: uniformly from all
-words satisfying the public Hamming parity checks. The exact one-solution result is
-stronger than the zero-hit sample, but neither estimates success under a model's
-structure-sensitive prior.
-
-## Oracle loop and G9 arms
-
-The revised bare run stopped at easy because every redraw was an API error. The four
-rows are preserved in `llm_loop_transcript.jsonl`; `solved="error"` is not a failure.
-
-| revised bare preset | valid attempts | solved | API errors | result |
-|---|---:|---:|---:|---|
-| easy (`n=31,N=15,t=11`) | 0 | 0 | 4 | unavailable; no verdict |
-
-The G9 copies contain only the shipping preset and were run in separate directories,
-as required.
-
-| G9 arm | solved / valid attempts | API errors | verdict |
-|---|---:|---:|---:|---|
-| bare | 0 / 0 | 4 | unavailable |
-| structural hint | 0 / 0 | 4 | unavailable |
-| placebo hint | 0 / 0 | 4 | unavailable |
-
-`hinted - placebo` is therefore undefined, so the arms support no conclusion about
-the declared invariant. The hint names only the global XOR invariant; it does not
-give the correction procedure. The size/effort part of G9 still passes: 66
-characters under the conservative token bound, 255 bit atoms, and 68 intended-route
-operations.
-
-## Use
-
-```python
-import importlib.util
-
-spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location(
-    "g", "results/2211.08812/gen_2211_08812.py"
-)
-g = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
-spec.loader.exec_module(g)
-
-inst = g.make_instance(seed=7, **g.DIFFICULTY[g.SHIPPING_DIFFICULTY])
-statement = g.render(inst)
-candidate = g.parse_answer("<answer>" + inst["answer"] + "</answer>")
-assert g.verify(inst, candidate) == (True, "ok")
-```
-
-From the repository root, after a valid oracle hardening run exists:
-
-```bash
-bash scripts/emit.sh 2211.08812 20 hard
-```
-
-## Caveats
-
-This is deliberately a Track B family and becomes easy with a sandbox: both majority
-and the hidden XOR route are fast. The 68-operation count treats a fixed-width
-255-bit XOR as one exact word operation; counting hexadecimal digits instead would
-make the manual route substantially longer, so the G9 interpretation is conservative
-only at the machine-word abstraction used by the module. The balanced construction
-has higher-order correlations; although raw weight, medoid, random restart,
-hex-digit plurality, and uncorrected XOR all fail, a stronger algebraic or learned
-distinguisher may expose them. SAT/SMT, information-set decoding, and belief
-propagation were not run because the disclosed majority algorithm already solves the
-class and belongs outside Track B's failing panel.
-
-The canonical key uses the complete multiset of per-row Hamming-distance profiles.
-It is invariant under every tested output reorder, codeword translation, and linear
-coordinate automorphism, but rare nonisomorphic output sets can share those profiles.
-No `gvlib` helper is needed: Python integer XOR, bit counts, and syndromes provide
-exact finite-field verification throughout. Most importantly, the cross-vendor
-no-tool claim remains unverified until OpenRouter quota is restored and all three
-arms are rerun.
