 ## Problem and trust model
 
-The source is Junnila, Laihonen, and Lehtilä, [*The Levenshtein's Sequence
-Reconstruction Problem and the Length of the List*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.08812).
-Section 1 defines the feasible list as `T(Y) = C ∩ ⋂_{y∈Y} B_t(y)`. An instance
-hands the solver a binary Hamming code `C` and an unordered set of distinct received
-words `Y`; the solver returns any codeword within Hamming distance `t` of every
-received word. Verification recomputes the GF(2) syndrome and every Hamming distance
-using exact integer operations. It accepts any valid witness and never reads the
-planted answer.
+The source is Junnila, Laihonen, and Lehtilä,
+[*The Levenshtein's Sequence Reconstruction Problem and the Length of the
+List*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.08812). Section 1 defines the feasible list as
+`T(Y) = C ∩ ⋂_{y∈Y} B_t(y)`. An instance supplies a binary repetition code `C`
+and a set of distinct received words `Y`; the solver returns a payload whose
+repeated codeword is within Hamming radius `t` of every received word.
 
-Generation is inverse, not solve-and-publish. A uniform codeword `x` is sampled
-first. The generator builds a binary error matrix with every row of weight exactly
-`t`. Every column has even weight strictly below half the channels except one
-near-half column of odd weight. Greedily assigning each column to the currently
-least-loaded rows makes all row weights equal; column parity proves that XORing every
-error row gives exactly the odd singleton. Hence all received words are exactly `t`
-from `x`, their bitwise majorities equal `x`, and XORing the odd number of received
-words gives a word one bit from `x`. The latter is corrected by the Hamming syndrome.
+Generation is inverse. A uniformly random payload and its codeword `x` are
+sampled first. Exact bipartite degree-sequence realizations then construct an
+error array in which every row has weight `t`, every physical column has a
+strict correct majority, and every logical coordinate has even error parity
+across the odd number of repetition blocks in each row. Thus all outputs are
+valid by construction, while XORing the blocks of any one output returns the
+payload. `verify` independently repeats a candidate and checks every Hamming
+distance; it accepts any valid witness and never reads `inst["answer"]`.
 
-Section 6, Theorem 28 supplies a second exact uniqueness argument. Its inequality
-with `k=e=1` holds on every tested instance, so every feasible codeword is within one
-bit of the same majority word. A distance-three Hamming code has at most one such
-codeword, while construction supplies one. This proves that shipping has exactly one
-valid answer without solving it.
+Uniqueness does not rely on sampling. Section 6, Theorem 28 says that its
+observable minority-count inequality places every feasible transmitted word
+within `k` of the same majority word. The generated inequality holds with
+`k=e=4`. The repetition code has minimum distance nine, so radius-four balls
+around its codewords are disjoint. Construction supplies one feasible word;
+therefore there is exactly one.
 
 ## Why Track B
 
-The paper proves information-theoretic list bounds, not computational hardness.
-Section 6 explicitly gives coordinate-wise majority in optimal `Theta(Nn)` time.
-At shipping, majority plus Hamming decoding performs 10,990 counted operations
-(10,965 channel-bit inspections plus syndrome work), solves 8/8 as expected, and
-took 0.0070 seconds total in the recorded run. Random independent corruptions would
-therefore be a false Track A family.
-
-The compact route is structural: XOR the 43 whole received words, compute eight
-parity checks, and correct the indicated coordinate. It uses 68 counted exact
-word-level operations. Without noticing the cancellation, the received bits are all
-close to tied and the solver must carry out the paper's mechanical majority scan.
-Easy regimes explicitly avoided are `t<=e` (Section 1 says one channel suffices) and
-pretending that the high-channel list bounds of Theorems 4 and 6, or the Section 6
-decoder, do not exist.
+Track A would be false. Section 6 explicitly gives coordinatewise majority in
+optimal `Theta(Nn)` time. Shipping contains 9,288 received bits. A stronger
+construction-aware implementation can majority only one 24-bit repetition
+block, but it still reads 1,032 bits; it solved 8/8 instances in about 0.0004
+seconds total. The paper's full scan costs 9,288 bit observations.
 
-An earlier sparse-error revision was genuinely trivial: a Gemini oracle solved the
-medium instance by hexadecimal-digit plurality, and that attack solved 100/100 old
-hard instances locally. Its script-owned evidence is preserved in
-`prebalance_llm_loop_transcript.jsonl`. The revised near-half columns reduce that
-same attack to 0/100 medium and 0/100 hard; it is now part of G6.
+The compact route is qualitatively different: notice that each row's nine error
+blocks XOR to zero, then XOR its nine six-hex-digit blocks. Because nine is odd,
+the codeword contribution is one copy of the payload. This takes 48
+single-hex-digit XORs. The easy regimes explicitly kept out of the hardness
+claim are `t<=e` (Section 1 says one channel suffices), the high-channel
+list-size regimes of Theorems 4 and 6, and the Section 6 decoder itself, which is
+reported as the successful Track-B reference rather than hidden in `attacks`.
 
 ## Worked demo
 
-For `seed=31415`, `n=15`, `N=11`, and `t=5`, the complete received set is:
